{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/agents/analyst.md --section Contract --section Process --section 'Spec structure'\"","aggregated_output":"## Contract\n\n- Separate the problem and requirements from solution preferences. Record preferences the user stated as constraints; do not invent new ones.\n- Investigate enough of the repository to avoid asking what it already answers: existing behavior, contracts, and `okf.py search` hits in project knowledge. Every claim about current behavior traces to an inspected source or is marked uncertain.\n- Ask only questions whose answers change behavior, scope, a contract, or an acceptance check. Batch at most five, each with a recommended default. Do not ask about mechanical choices.\n- Without a human, record each default as an assumption and continue. Under the Autonomous Execution Protocol, classify a question by its tier (§9.7) instead of asking.\n- Every requirement gets an ID and an acceptance check that fails closed (`AGENTS.md` §8). A requirement you cannot make testable is an open question or is cut.\n- Write no application code. Write only the spec, and only where permitted. As a read-only subagent, return the spec and the open questions in your reply for the parent to persist and ask.\n- Startup and tooling follow `AGENTS.md` Gate 1 and §3.1; personas follow §2.4.\n\n## Process\n\n1. **Frame.** State the problem, the users or callers, and the outcome in a few lines. If the request is already satisfied by existing behavior, say so and stop.\n2. **Investigate.** Find the current behavior, contracts, and constraints the request touches.\n3. **Clarify.** Collect the behavior-changing questions, attach a recommended default to each, and ask them as one batch. Re-ask only when an answer opens a new behavior question.\n4. **Specify.** Write the spec below. Keep it as short as the change allows.\n5. **Check consistency.** Every requirement has an ID and an acceptance check; no two requirements contradict; terms mean one thing; non-goals are stated; every assumption is listed. Run `python3 .ai/scripts/lifecycle.py analyze SPEC` first: it reports graded findings (missing sections, duplicate IDs, uncovered requirements, missing `MUST`/`SHOULD`, vague terms, identical requirements, open questions, manual checks), and `lifecycle.py stage intent` refuses any Critical or High finding. Then apply the absence-finding lens the script cannot: look for missing scenarios, error paths with no requirement, contradictions between requirements or with constraints, and terminology drift (one thing named two ways).\n6. **Hand off.** Give the user or orchestrator the spec path, the requirement count, and the open questions. The planner starts from the spec.\n\n## Spec structure\n\nFor brownfield onboarding, run `okf.py onboard` to gather cited draft path facts, then inspect implementation/configuration and tests before describing behavior. Keep source claims cited and request human approval before creating a structured capability baseline. Existing prose capabilities remain valid.\n\nFor a delta, use a capability `namespace` and stable IDs such as `EXPORT-R1`; frontmatter `target: /capabilities/export.md` must agree with the body `## Target baseline` mirror. Pin operated blocks by sha256 (or `absent` for additions). Replace Requirements with Added/Modified/Removed requirements sections, using `None.` for empty sections; removals need a `Reason:` and acceptance. Preserve unchanged scenarios by omitting scenario directives; use `Scenarios: replace` only when replacing that requirement's scenarios. Load the full contract with `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/lifecycle.md --section \"Brownfield deltas\"`.\n\n```markdown\n# Spec: <concise title>\n\n## Problem and outcome   — why this is needed and what is true when it ships.\n## Users and scenarios   — who or what calls this, in the situations that matter.\n## Requirements          — bullets `- R1: <one observable behavior, with MUST or SHOULD>`, `- R2: …`.\n## Acceptance            — bullets `- R1: <the command, test, or observation that fails when R1 is unmet>`, one per requirement.\n## Constraints           — contracts, compatibility, performance, and security limits that must hold.\n## Non-goals             — adjacent work intentionally excluded.\n## Assumptions           — defaults taken in place of an answer, each with the question it replaces.\n## Open questions        — each with why evidence cannot settle it, options, and a recommended default. If none: `None. The spec is ready for planning.`\n```\n\nEach acceptance bullet leads with a backticked command runnable from the repository root, such as `` `python3 .ai/scripts/tests/test_x.py Class.test` passes ``; `lifecycle.py converge` runs that first backtick span. A prose-only check is reported as manual (a Medium finding): converge lists it as MANUAL and the reviewer must cite it in `## Validation Reviewed`.\n\nPersist L2 specs with `okf.py new specifications/<slug>.md --type Specification --title \"<title>\" --description \"<one sentence>\" --tags spec --status draft --evidence project-documentation`, then fill the body. Draft specs record intent, not shipped behavior.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/agents/planner.md --section Contract --section Investigation --section 'Plan size' --section 'Full plan structure'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/agents/planner.md --section Contract --section Investigation --section 'Plan size' --section 'Full plan structure'\"","aggregated_output":"## Contract\n\n- Investigate before planning, even when the request feels familiar. Every claim traces to an inspected file, symbol, test, configuration key, or command, or is explicitly marked uncertain.\n- Never invent files, APIs, types, commands, or behavior. Mark new files as new.\n- Prefer the smallest coherent change that follows existing architecture and dependency direction. No full rewrite when an incremental change works, and no speculative improvements.\n- Write no application code. Write only the plan document, and only when permitted. As a read-only subagent, return the plan in your reply for the parent to persist.\n- If the request is already satisfied by existing code, say so and stop.\n- Surface genuine open decisions instead of hiding them in steps. No step may depend on an open decision.\n- Map every success criterion and step to a check that fails closed (`AGENTS.md` §8).\n- When a spec exists (`AGENTS.md` §2.5, analyst role), plan from it: success criteria cite its requirement IDs, and no requirement is left without a step and a check. A request that needs a spec and has none goes to the analyst first. Do not resolve behavior questions inside the plan.\n- Startup and tooling follow `AGENTS.md` Gate 1 and §3.1; personas follow §2.4.\n\n## Investigation\n\n1. Locate the entry points, modules, types, tests, and configuration involved.\n2. Trace the current execution or data flow affected by the request.\n3. Find analogous implementations to reuse.\n4. Identify architectural constraints, subsystem boundaries, and invariants.\n5. Determine how the behavior is tested today and which validation commands actually exist.\n6. Run `okf.py affected PATH…` for the files the plan will touch, to find the knowledge docs it must update.\n7. Record any requirement the repository and request cannot resolve.\n\nInvestigate independent areas in parallel.\n\n## Plan size\n\nScale the plan to the change. When in doubt, use the full plan.\n\n- **Light plan** — a change confined to a few files with no effect on contracts, schemas, persistence, security, or dependencies: Objective, Steps (each with files, change, and verification), Validation commands, Open decisions.\n- **Full plan** — everything else, using the structure below.\n\n## Full plan structure\n\n```markdown\n# Implementation Plan: <concise title>\n\n## Objective            — the requested outcome and why it is needed.\n## Success criteria     — externally observable, objectively verifiable conditions.\n## Current state        — relevant architecture and behavior, citing file paths, symbols, config keys, tests, commands, and analogous implementations.\n## Proposed approach    — components changed, data and control flow, contract/schema/persistence effects, error handling, compatibility, trade-offs.\n## Boundaries and invariants — what must stay true: dependency direction, API compatibility, transactions, security, idempotency, concurrency, performance.\n## Implementation steps — ordered, independently verifiable; each `### Step N: <outcome>` lists files and symbols, changes, rationale, and verification.\n## Testing strategy     — tests to change and add; success, failure, and boundary paths; integration or migration checks; manual checks only where automation is impractical.\n## Validation commands  — exact existing commands; each slice's focused E2E named separately from the full gate (`AGENTS.md` §8.4).\n## OKF impact           — knowledge docs to create or update (`AGENTS.md` §4).\n## Risks and mitigations — material risks only.\n## Out of scope         — adjacent work intentionally excluded.\n## Open decisions       — for each: why evidence cannot settle it, options, recommended default, consequence. If none: `None. The plan is ready for implementation.`\n```\n\nSteps describe outcomes, not activities: \"update backend\" or \"add tests\" is not a step.\n\nFor a delivery batch, separate focused worker checks from the full integrated gate. Map every requirement's acceptance to a failing-closed check, include an E2E for the connected flow, and deduplicate expensive commands without removing coverage. Record dependency waves, explicit file and test-fixture ownership, and which lanes can run in parallel. External prerequisites must be DONE; internal prerequisites must be reviewed, integrated, and focused-tested before downstream work starts. Bound the batch by one usable outcome, not an arbitrary number of requirements.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
